{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And to a woman in the impurity of her menstrual separation.</b> Scripture forbids the menstruant (niddah) for the reason I mentioned (Ramban on Leviticus 18:6), that the Torah permitted lying with a woman only for the perpetuation of seed. Now, the child is formed entirely or mostly from the blood of the woman, as I have already mentioned (Ramban on Leviticus 12:2), and from menstrual blood no child can be formed at all. How then could a child be made from it, when it is a deadly poison that kills any living creature that drinks or eats it? Furthermore, when there is much menstrual blood in the womb, she will not conceive from it at all, for it forms nothing. And even if she conceives from other blood and the fetus is nourished from this, it will kill it. Physicians have already noted further that if the fetus is nourished from superior blood, and all its sustenance is from good blood but some menstrual blood is absorbed into it, that blood will sour it and produce in the child boils and blisters of various kinds. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Tanhuma, Metzora 1), if any of it remains in his body, the child will be a metzora (leprous). In all these respects it is fitting that the Torah distance one from lying with a menstruant.\n\nThey have also reported a true test of it, and it is among the marvels of those perfect in knowledge concerning generation: for a menstruant, at the onset of her flow — if she gazes into a bright iron mirror and looks at length, drops of red like drops of blood will appear in the mirror, for the harmful evil nature within her produces an ugliness, and the corruption of the air clings to the mirror. She is thus like the basilisk that kills with its gaze, and all the more so she will harm the one who lies with her, for her body and her thought cling to him and to his thought. Therefore Scripture decreed concerning him (Leviticus 15:24), \"and her menstrual impurity shall be upon him,\" for her evil is an evil that adheres. This is what Scripture means by \"in the impurity of her menstrual separation\" (בְּנִדַּת טֻמְאָתָהּ), and what it means by \"the impurity of the menstruant\" (טֻמְאַת הַנִּדָּה) (Ezekiel 36:17) — for Scripture always associates impurity with her, for she is like a creeping thing and like a man afflicted with tzara'at, whose impurity is in their very bodies.\n\nIt is possible that this is also the reason for (Leviticus 20:18), \"her fountain shall be bared, and she has uncovered the source of her blood\" (אֶת מְקֹרָהּ הֶעֱרָה וְהִיא גִּלְּתָה אֶת מְקוֹר דָּמֶיהָ) — for that corrupting source ought to be sealed, not uncovered, so that they should draw from it the harmful, evil waters. And so it is forbidden for holy seed all the days of her impurity until she immerses in water, for then she is purified even in her thought and becomes completely clean."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The word אֶפְעֶה at the start of the comparison likely refers to a legendary creature (basilisk or cockatrice) said to kill with its gaze; rendered here according to its contextual sense in Ramban's argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:38.311587056Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}